    title: 'Accessible Web Design: Applying WCAG 2.2 to Real User Journeys',
    categories: ['Web Design', 'Digital Solutions', 'Digital Transformation'],
    keywords: ['accessible web design', 'WCAG 2.2', 'website accessibility', 'inclusive design', 'keyboard navigation', 'accessible forms'],
    reviewedOn,
    image: '/assets/images/insight-accessibility.webp',
    imageWidth: 1400,
    imageHeight: 875,
    imageAlt: 'Blind professional testing a website with a screen reader and UX specialist',
    summary: 'Accessible web design combines clear content, keyboard operation, readable contrast, adaptable layouts, and testing with people and assistive technologies.',
    sections: [
      { heading: 'Design for real tasks and varied ways of interacting', paragraphs: ['Start with the important journeys: finding a service, understanding eligibility, completing a form, downloading a document, or contacting the organization. Identify where users may encounter barriers and include keyboard, touch, screen-reader, low-vision, and cognitive considerations in design reviews.', 'The W3C Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.2 are the current W3C recommendation and apply across desktop, laptop, and mobile content. They provide testable success criteria, but meeting a checklist alone does not guarantee that every person can use a site.'] },
      { heading: 'Make the interface perceivable and understandable', paragraphs: ['Use sufficient text contrast, clear type hierarchy, descriptive headings, meaningful link labels, and text alternatives for informative images. Do not communicate a status or selection by color alone. Keep instructions and error messages close to the control they explain.', 'Design responsive pages so that content reflows without losing meaning or requiring unnecessary horizontal scrolling. Support text resizing and avoid placing essential text inside images. Keep titles and actions distinct so people can scan the page and understand what happens next.'] },
      { heading: 'Make every interaction operable', paragraphs: ['Ensure that core tasks work with a keyboard, show a visible focus indicator, and provide a logical focus order. Make controls large and spaced enough for touch, provide understandable labels, and avoid interactions that require dragging when an accessible alternative is practical.', 'Forms should identify required fields, explain expected formats, preserve entered information when possible, and describe errors in text. Test dialogs, menus, filters, and navigation with keyboard-only operation and assistive technology.'] },
      { heading: 'Test beyond automated scans', paragraphs: ['Automated tools can identify some issues, but they cannot assess every label, reading order, wording, or task barrier. Combine automated checks with keyboard walkthroughs, zoom and reflow checks, screen-reader testing, and evaluation by people with disabilities where feasible.', 'Include accessibility in design acceptance criteria and regression testing. Fixing a barrier early is generally less disruptive than discovering it after a redesign, procurement, or high-volume campaign has launched.'] },
      { heading: 'Treat accessibility as part of quality', paragraphs: ['Accessible design can improve clarity and usability for a wider audience, including people using mobile devices, older users, and visitors in constrained environments. It is not a decorative layer or an afterthought.', 'Identify the policy and legal obligations that apply to the organization and project. WCAG is a technical standard; appropriate counsel or accessibility specialists should advise on compliance obligations and formal conformance claims.'] },
    ],
